{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Then Yehudah approached him.</b> For he had no ability to remove every man from before him as Yosef had done." "<b>Yehudah.</b> Because he is the guarantor." "<b>In the ears of Adonai.</b> I wish to speak with you privately. And let not your anger burn against your servant, for one must indeed fear your wrath — because of <b>for you are like Pharaoh</b>: the two of you are equal in greatness, and I fear your anger, for to whom shall I complain about you? Therefore I need you to set your mind to my argument." "<b>For you are like Pharaoh.</b> An analogy: like me, like you; like a commoner, like a priest; like the good, like the sinner; like darkness, like light. Rashi: just as Pharaoh decrees and does not fulfill, etc., as is in the Midrash. It is written in the laws of Egypt that a slave does not rule, and he does not wear royal garments such as linen garments. And at the beginning of a king's reign they swear him in to uphold the laws of the land, and Pharaoh clothed you in linen garments; and just as we find that he does not fulfill his oath, he certainly does not keep his promise, which is nothing but mere speech — so too are you. Furthermore, Pharaoh proclaimed throughout all his land: whoever comes to buy grain, his coming and his going shall be in peace — this too he promises and does not do."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {3 "In the last clause, the text reads היינו מבטיח (lit. 'we are promising') where context requires היינו המלך מבטיח or similar ('we [i.e., the king] are promising'); the subject appears to have dropped or the form is corrupt. Translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:39.569297034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}